    html += f'          <tr><td>{row["country"]}</td><td class="num">{row["n_years"]}</td></tr>\n'

html += """        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title">Key Data Caveats</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> Not all countries report every year. The composition of reporting countries changes, meaning global aggregates are not strictly comparable across time. Early decades have fewer reporters.</li>
          <li><strong>Sex & age disaggregation:</strong> Sex-disaggregated data is available for fewer country-year pairs than overall rates. Age-disaggregated data is sparser still, limiting the robustness of demographic analyses.</li>
          <li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> UK data refer to England and Wales only, as reported in the original UNODC dataset. Scotland and Northern Ireland may appear as separate entries.</li>
          <li><strong>Territories:</strong> Some territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Puerto Rico) are reported separately and should not be double-counted with parent countries.</li>
          <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing data was not interpolated. Gaps in the time series reflect genuine absence of reporting, not estimation.</li>
          <li><strong>Reporting quality:</strong> National statistical capacity varies. Some countries may under-report homicides due to limited forensic infrastructure, conflict conditions, or definitional differences.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Policy Recommendations</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Based on the evidence presented in this report, we highlight the following policy-relevant findings and recommendations:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Targeted interventions in high-rate regions:</strong> The Americas, and parts of Africa, consistently record rates several times the global median. International cooperation frameworks and resource allocation should prioritize these regions for violence prevention programs.</li>
        <li><strong>Gender-specific strategies:</strong> The persistent male-to-female homicide rate ratio of approximately {fmt(mf_median, 1)}x calls for dual-track approaches: violence reduction programs targeting young male populations alongside specialized services for female victims of intimate partner and family violence.</li>
        <li><strong>Youth-focused prevention:</strong> Age-disaggregated data consistently shows peak victimization in the 15–29 and 30–44 age ranges. Evidence-based youth violence prevention, education, and economic opportunity programs should be central to national strategies.</li>
        <li><strong>Strengthen data infrastructure:</strong> The significant gaps in reporting — particularly for sex- and age-disaggregated data — limit evidence-based policy-making. Investment in national statistical systems and standardized reporting to UNODC should be a multilateral priority.</li>
        <li><strong>Monitor outlier trajectories:</strong> Countries showing rapid deterioration warrant immediate attention and assessment. Early warning systems incorporating homicide rate trends could help mobilize preventive responses before crises deepen.</li>
        <li><strong>Learn from success stories:</strong> Countries that have achieved dramatic reductions in homicide rates offer potential models for replication, though context-specific factors must be carefully considered in any knowledge transfer.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <h4>Final Assessment</h4>
    <p>Is the world becoming safer from intentional homicide? The data suggest a cautiously affirmative answer at the global level — median rates have trended downward over three decades. However, this improvement is neither universal nor guaranteed. Deep regional disparities persist, gender-based patterns of victimization remain entrenched, and reporting gaps mean the full picture is incomplete. For the communities and demographics most affected, the promise of a safer world remains unrealized. Sustained, evidence-based, and context-sensitive policy action is essential to translate aggregate trends into lived safety for all.</p>
